<p><strong>Boss&rsquo;s Day</strong> or National Boss&rsquo;s Day is celebrated each year on October 16 in the United States, Canada and some other countries and it was registered with the United States Chamber of Commerce in 1958 by Patricia Bays Haroski.&nbsp; She chose the date for the celebration as the 16th October because that was her boss&rsquo;s birthday.&nbsp; her boss also happened to be her father so we reckon she was slightly biased!&nbsp; LOL.&nbsp; The day was officially proclaimed Boss&rsquo;s Day in 1962 by the Governor of Illinois. The day was established to demonstrate appreciation and recognition for all good bosses out there and the tough roles and responsibilities that they hold.</p>
<h2><strong>Boss&rsquo;s Day Background</strong></h2>
<p>Boss&rsquo; Day was created by Patricia Bays Haroski in 1958 when she registered it with the <a href="https://www.uschamber.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">U.S. Chamber of Commerce </a>while working as a secretary at State Farm Insurance. The date she selected was her father&rsquo;s birthday, due in no small part to the fact that she was working for him at the time.</p>
<p>She felt that the work her father did was rarely recognized and that employees rarely showed appreciation for these people who kept their workplaces moving smoothly. By 1979 it had grown popular enough that Hallmark Cards issued a Boss&rsquo; Day card to honor the holiday.</p>
<h2><strong>Boss&rsquo;s Day Celebrations </strong></h2>
<p>There is a power differential between employees and employers, where the employer generally makes more money and has more power overall, and thus it has been posited that Boss&rsquo; Day should not be observed and that any gift-giving in the workplace should flow down.</p>
<p>It&rsquo;s an interesting debate, but we feel that it&rsquo;s important to recognize that Bosses are people too, and while they may be in a position of greater power, they also have greater responsibility, and their efforts are what gives employees continuing work.</p>
<p>Perhaps both Boss and Employee need to work together to create an environment of teamwork in the workplace, and Boss&rsquo; Day can be a good step in that direction.<strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>
<h2><strong>Boss&rsquo;s Day Customs and Traditions</strong></h2>
<p>Celebrate Boss&rsquo; Day by giving them a nice card, or just stop in and let them know how much you appreciate what they do. If you realize that you don&rsquo;t really know what their work consists of or what demands are placed on them, Boss&rsquo; Day can also be a good opportunity to learn exactly what they do each day to keep your workplace moving smoothly.</p>
<p>Boss\'s Day is meant to be a day for employees to appreciate their supervisors, and to provide an opportunity for bosses and employees to improve their relationships. Boss\'s Day is also known as National Boss Day, but it is not a nationwide public holiday.<strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>
